<hr> 
<h3> About me
</h3>

<P class="blocktext">

I am an Associate Professor in the School of Mathematics at the University of Minnesota,
Twin Cities. From 2015 to 2018, I was a postdoc in Amit Singer's research group
in the Program in Applied and Computational Mathematics at Princeton University,
where I was supported by the Simons Collaboration on Algorithms and Geometry.
In 2015, I completed my Ph.D in Mathematics at Yale University,
working under the supervision of Ronald Coifman.
In 2010, I earned my B.S. in Mathematics from the University of Chicago.

</P>







<hr>
<h3> Research
</h3>

<P class="blocktext">

My research interests are in applied and computational harmonic analysis,
statistical signal processing, and high-dimensional statistics.
Specifically, I develop algorithms for processing data corrupted by geometric deformations,
linear filters, and high additive noise.
</P>



